# ─── Agent runtime state ─────────────────────────────────────────
.pi/
.atl/
.life/

# ─── Stray paths from shell-quote bugs ──────────────────────────
# Literal-tilde dirs (e.g. `~/` typed in a path that was replayed
# as a literal string → `src/ikigai/~/.ikigai/*.db` was tracked).
# These are NOT user-home expansions; treat `~` as a path component.
**/~/**
**/~*.db
# Stray zero-byte / one-char / shell-quote-garbage files at repo root.
# These came from malformed bash redirection (e.g. `> agent('Execute')`).
# Listed individually because gitignore glob character classes can't
# reliably match quotes/backticks/brackets across shells.
# Examples seen 2026-08-28: '  `  -  0  6.0  None  For  d.classification  list[object]  s.docs
# Examples seen 2026-08-29: '  2  6  None  com  dict[str  formal  one  str  Algorítmica
# Root-level bare digits (bash > digit bugs)
/[0-9]
# Root-level files starting with shell-special chars (', [, {, `)
/['\[\{`]
# Root-level Python keyword / short-token names that appear from
# malformed redirect targets. Listed individually — gitignore does
# not support alternation `(a|b)` or braces `{a,b}` in match patterns.
/None
/str
/int
/float
/bool
/list
/dict
/com
/formal
/one
# Root-level type-annotation fragments (e.g. `dict[str`, `list[object]`).
# `\[` matches one literal `[`; `*` matches the rest. Without `*` the
# pattern would only match the prefix without the bracket content.
/dict\[*
/list\[*
/tuple\[*
/set\[*
/frozenset\[*
# Examples seen 2026-08-29: comma+plus literals, capitalized paths,
# parens-wrapped calls, backtick fragments, single lowercase words
# from bash redirects like `> Path`, `> emit`, `> no`, `> frontmatter.dumps()`.
/0,+,+
/Path
/emit
/no
/frontmatter.dumps()
/str\`
# Examples seen 2026-09-03: short English conjunctions / short common words
# from bash redirects like `> but`, `> and`, `> or`, `> not`, `> if`.
# UTF-8 mojibake filenames (e.g. `n??o` where `?` is U+FFFD replacement char)
# are NOT matched by these literal entries — those need cleanup at the
# source by ensuring bash redirection uses `cmd 2>/dev/null > file.txt`
# rather than bare `> word`. See memory `windows-orphan-dir-delete.md`
# and `bash-heredoc-at-leak.md` for the broader heredoc-redirect problem.
/but
/and
/or
/not

